        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">What a healthy Google Business Profile actually requires</h2>
        <p>
          Healthy, on this page, means the record is real, current, and the
          same as the site. It does not mean a position. The profile is one of
          the places Google and AI assistants read your business facts. If
          those facts are wrong, that is what gets read.
        </p>
        <p>What we treat as healthy:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The real business name. Not a name padded with search terms.</li>
          <li>Categories that match the work you actually do. Not a thin list, and not a padded one.</li>
          <li>Hours that match the hours you keep, including when those hours change.</li>
          <li>Name, address, hours, and license in agreement with the site.</li>
          <li>Services listed the same way on the profile and on the site.</li>
          <li>Questions answered, not left sitting.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          This is an operating standard, not a legal opinion, and not a reading
          of a ranking system. We do not publish a checklist score. We have
          not scored NWA profiles, and we will not invent one.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Why NWA profiles underperform</h2>
        <p>
          A profile underperforms, on this page, when a buyer cannot tell what
          you do, when you are open, or whether anyone is there. That is a
          record problem. It is not a claim about where the profile will sit.
        </p>
        <p>
          The usual misses are the same in Fayetteville, Bentonville, Rogers,
          Springdale, and Fort Smith. This page does not publish a share of
          local profiles that fail this way. We have not counted them.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Thin categories.</strong> The profile names one service and
          hides the rest, or it names work the shop does not do. A thin list
          is an incomplete record. A padded list is a false one. Neither is a
          growth tactic.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Stale hours.</strong> The profile still shows last season's
          hours, or a holiday closure nobody removed. A buyer who trusts that
          record shows up when you are closed, or does not show up when you
          are open. The facts are wrong either way.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Unanswered questions.</strong> Someone asked something on the
          profile and nobody replied. The question sits there as part of the
          record. A healthy profile does not leave it sitting. We do not
          publish a response-time promise. The price sheet does not have one.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">The five fixes, and which ones touch the profile</h2>
